WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:08.700
Every book has a biological story to tell.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.210
Much more than what is written on the page.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.280
The big question is with ancient books-

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.280
rare and delicate objects --

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.520
how do we access this buffet of biological clues,

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.040
without damaging them?

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.400
A team of researchers is employing

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.400
an array of non-destructive methods

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.960
to read the biological history of ancient books.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.500
They focussed their analysis on

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.680
an exceptionally well preserved medieval manuscript,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.560
a 12th century copy of the Gospel of Luke.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.650
Examining the cover, scientists observed tiny holes

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.649
that went straight through the leather.

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:44.180
Measurements indicated that these holes were created

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.510
by the tunneling larvae

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.440
of Anobium punctatum, a northern European beetle.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.879
This confirms that the book was made in England

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.460
or Northern Europe rather than in southern Europe

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.600
where a different type of beetle infested books.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.100
Next, researchers used a new method

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.800
to gently tease away tiny samples of the leather cover.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.360
An eraser was gently applied to the surface.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.460
The rubbing creates friction

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.360
which loosens the material’s fibers.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.260
And the resulting eraser debris

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.340
includes strands of proteins from the leather.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.380
A mass spectrometer isolated the amino acids

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.380
from these samples.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.700
Then researches were able to determine

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.340
what species of animal the leather came from.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.130
The binding was identified as Roe deer.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.860
The strap as either Fallow or Red deer.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.860
This gives us a glimpse at wildlife management

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.220
in 12th century England

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.660
when native roe deer seem to have declined

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.160
and landowners were stocking their parks instead

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.240
with larger deer species,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.900
who were probably introduced by the Normans.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.400
Inside the book, the eraser technique was applied

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.840
to each page of parchment,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.789
which is made of animal skins.

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:50.720
Protein analysis shows that the pages were made

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.000
not from just calves, as originally thought,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.160
but also from &nbsp;sheep and a bit of goat.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.120
Previously, it was thought that goatskin

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.340
was used for parchment

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.820
only in Southern Europe and rarely in England--

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.689
only in poor areas as a last resort.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:05.680
Another book dating from this time period

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.270
and from the same location

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.480
also was found to be made of

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:09.880
more than one type of animal.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.160
This suggests that scribes sometimes faced shortages

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.980
of certain animals and had to make do

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.520
with what was on hand.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.840
Researchers are starting to use the same

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:18.860
erasing technique to test

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.140
not just for proteins but also DNA.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.040
Looking at a third ancient book from the same location

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.120
as the Gospel of Luke, they found that

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.100
20% of the DNA extracted came from humans.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.500
Especially on pages containing oaths,

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.610
which would’ve been read, kissed, and touched

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.280
more than other parts of the book.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.960
They also identified two microbes

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.680
intimately connected with people:

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.840
Propionibacterium, which causes acne

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.500
and Staphylococcus, which includes many strains

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.860
that cause staph and other infections.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.599
With this array of new tools, researchers hope to build

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:53.409
a global parchment DNA library that includes

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:56.770
DNA from microbes, humans, and animals.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.500
As more manuscripts are read

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.260
for their biological history,

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.480
the whole story of the medieval world of scribes, monks,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.540
and readers who touched these ancient books

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.700
can be told.

